# The Fano plane, the smallest projective plane and the multiplication table of the octonions. It has seven points and
# seven lines, each line holding three points and each point on three lines, the most symmetric incidence a finite
# geometry can have. It is the projective plane over the two-element field, its seven points the nonzero patterns of three
# bits. It carries a SINGER CYCLE, a single rotation that permutes all seven points in one orbit and carries lines to
# lines, so the whole structure has a seven-fold cyclic symmetry. This file MODELS the seven points and DEFINES the Singer
# rotation, then PROVES it has order seven, seven turns return every point, so the cyclic symmetry is exact. The point
# count as a projective count and the flag count are recorded as machine-integer facts. Labeling the seven points with the
# imaginary octonion units makes each of the seven lines a quaternion triple. The order theorem is proved over the actual
# point type by induction.

# The seven points of the Fano plane, permuted in one orbit by the Singer cycle.
form point
  case point-zero
  case point-one
  case point-two
  case point-three
  case point-four
  case point-five
  case point-six

# The Singer rotation, cycling all seven points in a single orbit and carrying lines to lines.
task shift
  take p, like point
  like point
  fork case, read p
    case point-zero
      send back
        make point-one
    case point-one
      send back
        make point-two
    case point-two
      send back
        make point-three
    case point-three
      send back
        make point-four
    case point-four
      send back
        make point-five
    case point-five
      send back
        make point-six
    case point-six
      send back
        make point-zero

# THE SINGER CYCLE HAS ORDER SEVEN: seven turns return every point, shift applied seven times is the identity, for every
# point. So the Fano plane has a seven-fold cyclic symmetry permuting its points in one orbit. Proved by induction over
# the seven points.
rule the-singer-cycle-has-order-seven
  seat p, like point
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call shift
        call shift
          call shift
            call shift
              call shift
                call shift
                  call shift
                    read p
      read p
  fold p

# THE POINT COUNT is two to the third minus one, seven, the nonzero vectors of three-bit space, recorded in addition-only
# form: 4 + 2 + 1 = 7.
hold fano-plane-has-seven-points
  call is-equal
    code 7
    call add
      code 4
      call add
        code 2
        code 1

# THE FLAGS, the point-on-line incidences, count both ways: seven points times three lines each is seven lines times three
# points each, 7 * 3 = 21, and this is also the number of pairs of points, each pair on exactly one line.
hold fano-flags-count-twenty-one
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 7
      code 3
    code 21
